{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791541729,"nonce":"O84RmoQ+q5h60fCME7UpzXe/iE8=","title":"Libraries: wire pending-error globals into compiler analysis","message":"Lua does not call TieredRuntime::set_error_flag_global for zl_pending, so its pending-error checks never reach the compiler error_flag analysis. Imported prelowered globals are also copied without applying LoweringConfig::error_flag_global; exposing a configured global by declaration returns early before the usual marker is set. Snapshots lowered without a language's runtime configuration therefore keep error_flag=false after linking, including Python's configured pending exception.\n\nApply the runtime configuration when adopting and exposing snapshot globals, preserving recorded markers. Register Lua's pending global. Library functions may be entered during error reporting/unwinding and must preserve that entry state rather than assuming the flag is clear.\n\nValidate eager/deferred imports, explicit and implicit exposure, preexisting markers, unrelated globals, protected calls, uncaught errors, close/finalizer paths and both native tiers. Compare frozen LLVM-off binaries against 54e54e06 on ARM64 and the NUC with no builds during timing. Do not infer a performance gain from the metadata change alone.","files":null}]}